    "https://strayer.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/ANZ_TUA_External1/job/Campus-Surry-Hills/Student-Complaints-Officer_R31112": {
      "title": "Student Complaints Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nWho we are?   At Torrens University Australia, making a difference is in our DNA. We believe in the transformative power of education to change lives, families, communities – and the world. That’s why we’re on a mission to increase access to higher education, so learners from all walks of life can chase their dreams.     Just like our students, our employees are a diverse group who want to make an impact. When you join us, we give you the freedom to be bold, creative, and courageous – with flexible working arrangements that allow you to bring your best. We’ve built a culture that celebrates community, collaboration, and innovation, where people love what they do.     What’s the job?   As the Student Complaints Officer , you’ll support the Student Complaints Manager to manage and resolve a significant volume of student complaints and appeals, including matters relating to academic integrity and generative AI. You’ll help ensure each matter is handled fairly, consistently and transparently, while keeping students informed and maintaining accurate , auditable records.     You’ll investigate complaints and appeals, assess evidence, prepare clear case summaries and recommendations, and draft outcome correspondence in plain English. You’ll provide practical advice on the Student Complaints Policy and related procedures, work with academic and professional teams on complex matters, and contribute to improvements that strengthen confidence in the University’s complaint-handling processes.     Principal accountabilities include (but are not limited to):   Triage new appeals, set priorities and maintain momentum across a high-volume caseload.   Gather and test evidence, liaise with relevant parties, verify policy application and assess appropriate remedies .   Prepare accurate reports, succinct case summaries, recommendations and plain-English outcome letters.   Keep complainants and appellants informed about processes, timelines and outcomes, de-escalating concerns where possible.   Identify and refer urgent welfare or safety risks through established pathways.   Apply policies and procedures consistently, ensuring procedural fairness, independence and the absence of conflicts of interest.   Meet acknowledgement and resolution timeframes , recording defensible reasons for any extensions.   Safeguard privacy and confidentiality and maintain accurate records in approved records management systems.   Provide specialist advice on appeals, including academic integrity breaches and generative AI use.   Work with the Student Complaints Manager, Policy and Academic Governance, Legal and relevant offices on complex or high-risk matters.   Contribute to complaint-handling resources, training, benchmarking and continuous improvement activities.   Build respectful relationships with students, academics and professional staff while delivering outstanding service.     Who you are :   An experienced complaints or case management professional with at least five years’ experience managing student complaints, preferably within a university or comparable education environment.   Confident handling appeals and complaints involving academic integrity issues, including the use of generative AI.   Highly organised and detail-focused, with the ability to manage a high-volume workload accurately and efficiently.   A clear and empathetic communicator who can write concise, well-reasoned correspondence for diverse audiences, including people who speak English as an additional language.   Knowledgeable in administrative policies, procedures, privacy, procedural fairness and records management.   Collaborative and resilient, with a student-service mentality and the judgement to balance people, compliance and organisational needs.   Technically capable, including strong skills in word processing, spreadsheets and enterprise records management systems.   An undergraduate-qualified professional, or someone with equivalent relevant experience.     To read more about the role please click here .   This is a full-time permanent role based at our Surry Hills  campus in Sydney . Offering hybrid flexibility to work from home or allocated campus days .     Why join us?   Bold, modern and agile, we’re Australia’s fastest-growing university, going from 164 students in 2014 to 40,000+ alumni across 115+ countries and 2,000+ staff today. In 2020, Australian Financial Review named us one of the country’s most innovative companies. As we continue to establish ourselves as a truly global university, we never lose sight of our student-centric approach. Supported by teams of passionate people, you’ll find opportunities for professional development and career progression.     We are proud to be an equal opportunity employer and committed to creating an inclusive workplace. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, colour, religion, age, ethnicity, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, or any other protected characteristic.     We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all positions.     What we offer:   We support your personal passions, development and wellbeing, to make your working life a more rewarding experience. At Torrens University Australia, you’ll benefit from:   Study free in a course or degree related to your position   Access to internal opportunities – be supported to learn, grow and move across the organisation   A values-driven and purpose-led working environment   Hiring process :   We aim to provide you with the information you need at each stage of this process to help you present your best self.     If you have any accessibility requirements, please contact us at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au for confidential support. We are committed to ensuring an equitable , barrier-free application process and have a Reasonable Adjustment Procedure in place to support you throughout the recruitment process.     To learn more about what makes Torrens University Australia a great place to work, visit https://www.torrens.edu.au/blog.   We’d love to hear from you.     Job reference: R31112     Applications for this role will close on Wednesday 14 October.     Please note, we may close applications earlier should a suitable candidate be identified . You can expect to hear from our Talent Acquisition team after this date with updates regarding your application.   If you require a reasonable accommodation to complete our application process, please contact our Human Resources Department at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au"
    },
    "https://strayer.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/ANZ_TUA_External1/job/Campus-Surry-Hills/Work-Health-and-Safety-Manager_R31159-1": {
      "title": "Work Health and Safety Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nWho we are?   At Torrens University Australia, making a difference is in our DNA. We believe in the transformative power of education to change lives, families, communities – and the world. That’s why we’re on a mission to increase access to higher education, so learners from all walks of life can chase their dreams.   Just like our students, our employees are a diverse group who want to make an impact. When you join us, we give you the freedom to be bold, creative, and courageous – with flexible working arrangements that allow you to bring your best. We’ve built a culture that celebrates community, collaboration, and innovation, where people love what they do.   What’s the job?   As the Work Health and Safety Manager , you’ll lead the planning, implementation and continuous improvement of work health and safety across Torrens Global Education and its related entities. Acting as a trusted consultant to all levels of staff, you’ll promote safe working practices, strengthen awareness and help ensure compliance with legislation and workplace obligations.   You’ll partner with business units and leaders to identify and manage physical and psychological risks, provide practical advice and maintain effective health and safety frameworks. The role also manages work-related and non-work-related injuries, workers compensation claims, reporting, training, incident investigations and WHS projects across the organisation.   You’ll help foster a culture of safety awareness and accountability by encouraging open communication, timely reporting and participation in safety initiatives. Working closely with HR, campus, risk and project teams, you’ll support measurable safety improvements and maintain the organisation’s readiness to meet its regulatory and compliance responsibilities.   Principal accountabilities include (but are not limited to):   Partner with business units to provide advice on safe workplace obligations, frameworks, tools and legislation, helping teams mitigate physical and psychological risks.   Manage work-related and non-work-related injuries end to end and effectively manage workers compensation claims to support employees and mitigate organisational risk.   Manage the annual renewal of workers compensation premiums, assess cost drivers and identify opportunities for improvement.   Develop, coordinate and maintain WHS frameworks, policies, records and information across the organisation.   Monitor legislative changes and maintain ongoing compliance with WHS requirements, including relevant regulatory standards and codes of practice.   Develop and deliver health and safety training for key stakeholders.   Collaborate with department managers to investigate accidents, incidents and near misses and recommend corrective actions to prevent recurrence.     Who you are   A WHS professional with qualifications in work health and safety and/or at least six years’ experience in this specialist area.   A holder of a Return to Work Coordinator certificate for South Australia.   Experienced in applying WHS legislation, regulations and codes of practice in a complex organisation.   Experienced in managing work-related injuries, workers compensation claims and end-to-end case management.   A confident project lead with experience delivering work health and safety initiatives and practical improvements.   A strong communicator and relationship builder who can consult effectively with leaders, employees and diverse stakeholders.   Highly organised and self-directed, with the ability to manage competing priorities and day-to-day workflow under broad supervision.   To read more about the role please click here .   This is a full-time, permanent position offering hybrid flexibility, with the opportunity to work from home and from one of our campuses. Preferred location Sydney or Melbourne we are also open to Adelaide and Brisbane location for this role.   Why join us?   Bold, modern and agile, we’re Australia’s fastest-growing university, going from 164 students in 2014 to 40,000+ alumni across 115+ countries and 2000+ staff today. In 2020, Australian Financial Review named us one of the country’s most innovative companies. As we continue to establish ourselves as a truly global university, we never lose sight of our student-centric approach. Supported by teams of passionate people, you’ll find opportunities for professional development and career progression.   We are proud to be an equal opportunity employer and committed to creating an inclusive workplace. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, colour, religion, age, ethnicity, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, or any other protected characteristic.   We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all positions.   What we offer:   We support your personal passions, development and wellbeing, to make your working life a more rewarding experience. At Torrens University Australia, you’ll benefit from:   Study free in a course/degree related to your position   Make a measurable impact on employee wellbeing and workplace safety   A purpose-driven workplace that values community, collaboration and innovation   Hiring process   We aim to provide you with the information you need at each stage of this process to help you present your best self.   If you have any accessibility requirements, please contact maur.bell@torrens.edu.au for confidential support. We are committed to ensuring an equitable, barrier-free application process and have a Reasonable Adjustment Procedure in place to support you throughout the recruitment process.   To learn more about what makes Torrens University Australia a great place to work, visit https://www.torrens.edu.au/blog.   We’d love to hear from you.   Job reference: R31159   Applications for this role will close on Sunday 18th October. If you require a reasonable accommodation to complete our application process, please contact our Human Resources Department at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au"
    },
    "https://strayer.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/ANZ_TUA_External1/job/Campus-Surry-Hills/Senior-Project-Manager_R31184-1": {
      "title": "Senior Project Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSenior Project Manager  Fixed-Term | Sydney | Hybrid flexible working Who We Are   At Torrens University Australia, making a difference is in our DNA. As a Certified B Corporation, we belong to a global community of future-focused companies using business as a force for good. We believe in the transformative power of education to change lives, families, communities – and the world. That’s why we’re on a mission to increase access to higher education, so learners from all walks of life can chase their dreams. Just like our students, our employees are a diverse group who want to make an impact. When you join us, we give you the freedom to be bold, creative and courageous – with flexible working arrangements that allow you to bring your best. We’ve built a culture that celebrates community, collaboration and innovation, where people love what they do.  What’s the Job? We are seeking an experienced Senior Project Manager to lead the integrated delivery of the University’s Banner SaaS implementation and associated business transformation programme. This is a high-profile, enterprise-wide role that will bring together University teams, implementation partners, system integrators and third-party vendors. You will establish the structure, governance and delivery discipline required to move a complex programme forward and support successful implementation across the University. You will lead programme planning, governance, reporting, risk and dependency management, vendor coordination, testing, cutover and operational readiness. You will work closely with accountable functional, technical, data, integration, testing, training and change leads to ensure all parts of the programme are aligned and progressing. You will not be expected to be the technical subject-matter expert across every domain. However, you will need sufficient understanding of enterprise systems, integrations, data and technology delivery to identify risks, challenge assumptions and coordinate effective decisions. This is a 12 months fixed-term opportunity based at our Surry Hills campus in Sydney, with hybrid flexibility. Key Responsibilities (but not limited to): Lead a foundational platform / programme plan, including milestones, dependencies, resources, decisions and critical path activities.  Align programme delivery with university priorities, expected benefits, portfolio planning, and organisational outcomes. Establish and operate effective governance, executive reporting, assurance, and escalation processes. Coordinate functional, technical, data, integration, security, testing, training and change workstreams. Manage scope, requirements, risks, issues, dependencies, change control, and formal approvals. Act as the University’s primary day-to-day contact for implementation partners, system integrators and third-party vendors. Monitor vendor deliverables, performance, resourcing, service levels, commercial obligations, and escalations. Coordinate migration, technical readiness, and dependencies across the University’s wider digital ecosystem. Lead integrated testing, cutover and operational-readiness planning, ensuring workstream owners are prepared for implementation. Support regulatory compliance, benefits realisation, continuous improvement and the uplift of project delivery capability across the University. Who You Are   You are a calm, structured and outcomes-focused programme professional who is comfortable working in complex, matrixed environments. You bring clarity to competing priorities, communicate complex issues plainly and build confidence through transparency and follow-through. You will bring: Demonstrated experience leading complex enterprise technology, SaaS, student information system or business transformation programmes. Direct Ellucian Banner experience is highly desirable. Experience with another higher education platform or large-scale enterprise system will also be considered. Strong experience in programme planning, governance, risk, issue, dependency and change management. Demonstrated experience coordinating system integration, data migration, testing, cutover and operational readiness across multiple workstreams. Proven experience managing implementation partners, system integrators and third-party vendors, including delivery commitments, performance, resourcing and escalations. Strong stakeholder-management and communication skills, with the ability to influence executives, academic leaders, technical specialists, operational teams and external partners. Experience working in a matrix environment with distributed or international teams. The ability to make sound decisions with incomplete information, challenge constructively and maintain momentum through ambiguity. Relevant project or programme management qualifications. PMP, PRINCE2, MSP or an equivalent certification would be highly regarded. Why Join Us? This is an opportunity to lead a significant enterprise transformation with genuine organisational reach. You will work across technology, operations, academic and leadership teams while helping shape the platforms and digital ecosystem that support the University’s future. At Torrens University Australia, you will benefit from: A purpose-led, B Corp-certified organisation. Flexible hybrid working arrangements. The opportunity to lead a complex, high-profile transformation with global stakeholders. Access to professional development and internal career opportunities. The opportunity to study free in a course related to your position, subject to eligibility requirements. A collaborative and inclusive working environment. Our Commitment to Inclusion We are proud to be an equal opportunity employer and are committed to creating an inclusive, accessible and equitable workplace. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and people from all backgrounds. If you require adjustments or additional support during the recruitment process, please contact yourcareer@torrens.edu.au for confidential assistance. To learn more about what makes Torrens University Australia a great place to work, visit https://www.torrens.edu.au/blog . We’d love to hear from you.     Job reference: R31184 To ensure a fair and thorough review process, applications for this role will close on Sunday 18 October. You can expect to hear from our Talent Acquisition team after this date with any updates about your applications. If you require a reasonable accommodation to complete our application process, please contact our Human Resources Department at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au"
    },
    "https://strayer.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/ANZ_TUA_External1/job/Campus-Surry-Hills/Accountant_R31155-2": {
      "title": "Accountant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nWho we are? At Torrens University Australia , making a difference is in our DNA. As a Certified B Corporation, we belong to a global community of future-focused companies using business as a force for good. We believe in the transformative power of education to change lives, families, communities – and the world. That’s why we’re on a mission to increase access to higher education, so learners from all walks of life can chase their dreams.     Just like our students, our employees are a diverse group who want to make an impact. When you join us, we give you the freedom to be bold, creative and courageous – with flexible working arrangements that allow you to bring your best. We’ve built a culture that celebrates community, collaboration and innovation, where people love what they do .   What’s the job? Reporting to the Senior Financial Accountant, you will assist with the preparation of month-end journals and reconciliations across various areas, including banking, revenue, receivables and other financial statement line items as required.   You will also assist with other month-end and quarterly tasks, including the preparation of disclosure packages and analytics. Why join the team? You’ll join a strong, values-driven finance team known for its collaboration, capability and supportive culture. This role offers exposure beyond core accounting, partnering with teams such as Business Intelligence and other finance teams, while contributing to process improvements, automation and smarter ways of working.  Day-to-day accountabilities: Preparation of timely and accurate journal entries and reconciliations. Assistance with preparation of supplementary monthly and quarterly close deliverables. Assistance with periodic internal and external audit requirements. Assistance with ad hoc financial requests as required. Adherence to internal control (SOX) requirements, including maintenance of critical reports and spreadsheets.   To read more about the role please click here to view job description .   This is a 12-month fixed-term position located at our Surry Hills, Sydney Campus.   Who you are A Bachelor of Accounting or a similar qualification. Experience in finance month-end close processes. Strong attention to detail. Ability to work independently. Excellent communication, analytical and organisational skills. Strong problem-solving skills. Integrity and strong business ethics. Advanced Excel (would be an advantage). CA or CPA designation (would be an advantage). PeopleSoft (would be an advantage).   Why join us? Bold, modern and agile, we’re Australia’s fastest-growing university, going from 164 students in 2014 to 40,000+ alumni across 115+ countries and 2000+ staff today. In 2020, Australian Financial Review named us one of the country’s most innovative companies. As we continue to establish ourselves as a truly global university, we never lose sight of our student-centric approach. Supported by teams of passionate people, you’ll find endless opportunities for professional development and career progression.     We are proud to be an equal opportunity employer and committed to creating an inclusive workplace. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, colour, religion, age, ethnicity, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, or any other protected characteristic.     We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all positions.   Hiring process We aim to provide you with the information you need at each stage of this process to help you present your best self. If you have any accessibility requirements, please contact your recruiter at maur.bell@torrens.edu.au for confidential support. We are committed to ensuring an equitable, barrier-free application process and have a Reasonable Adjustment Procedure in place to support you throughout the recruitment process.     To learn more about what makes Torrens University Australia a great place to work, visit https://www.torrens.edu.au/blog .  Applications close: 15th October 2026   Job reference: R31155 If you require a reasonable accommodation to complete our application process, please contact our Human Resources Department at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au"
    },
    "https://strayer.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/ANZ_TUA_External1/job/Campus-Surry-Hills/Academic-Registrar-and-Senior-Director-Academic-Services_R31166-1": {
      "title": "Academic Registrar and Senior Director Academic Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nWho we are:   At Torrens University Australia , making a difference is in our DNA. As a Certified B Corporation, we belong to a global community of future-focused companies using business as a force for good. We believe in the transformative power of education to change lives, families, communities – and the world. That’s why we’re on a mission to increase access to higher education, so learners from all walks of life can chase their dreams.    A progressive university, we ground our studies in industry. Expert academics bring their insights into the classroom, with a range of assessment types that aim to be authentic and informed by industry needs. Bridging the gap between study and work, our educators prepare students for career success and connect them with future employers. Building relevant skills, experience and networks from day one, students don’t just learn about their industry, they live it.    What’s the job?   The Academic Registrar and Senior Director Academic Services is the university's senior leader for Academic Services. The role provides strategic leadership across the university's academic services ecosystem, setting portfolio-wide direction, service standards, planning frameworks, assurance and performance expectations.  The role leads student academic services and academic administration across the student lifecycle, including academic records, progression and completion, graduations, subject and course logistics, academic systems, complaints and appeals, student safety and wellbeing compliance, academic data processing and support for academic enquiries.  You will be accountable for the integrity, security, accuracy and effectiveness of academic records, student administration, academic governance, planning, complaints, appeals, student information systems, data and delivery readiness. The role connects three portfolios: student governance, compliance and academic services assurance; academic governance and administration; and academic planning and operations.    Principle accountabilities (but not limited to):   Senior Leadership and Portfolio Governance - lead Academic Services by setting strategic priorities, service standards, accountabilities and performance expectations across the three-portfolio operating model.  Academic Governance, Policy and Quality - lead academic governance and quality processes, ensuring academic rules, regulatory requirements, reporting frameworks and escalation pathways support sound decisions and continuous improvement.  Registrar, Complaints, Appeals and Independent Assurance - provide senior oversight of the assurance function, complaints, appeals, procedural fairness and student wellbeing and safety risks while protecting independence and impartiality.  Records, Results, Completions and Graduation - oversee the accuracy, security and integrity of student outcomes, academic records, results, completions, certification, conferral and graduation processes across the student lifecycle  Academic Planning, Timetabling and Delivery Readiness - lead integrated academic planning, timetabling and delivery readiness using demand, capacity, progression and workforce data to identify risks and support timely decisions.  Systems, Data and Continuous Improvement - drive continuous improvement across Academic Services systems, processes, data and workflow by strengthening automation, data quality, controls, integrations, security and vendor delivery.  Who you are:   Senior leader with extensive higher education, VET or dual-sector experience across academic services, registry, governance, planning and operations.  Experienced in academic planning, forecasting, timetabling, delivery readiness, systems, data and process improvement.  Experienced in overseeing student wellbeing and safety, including sensitive matters, high-risk issues and regulatory obligations.  Trusted adviser who can influence senior leaders and governance bodies across complex organisational structures.  Accomplished people leader skilled in building high-performing teams, accountability and organisational change.  Strong knowledge of the Australian tertiary regulatory environment, including HESF, TEQSA, ASQA, ESOS, the National Code, CRICOS, VET, privacy and data requirements.  Strong institutional judgement and data-led capability in assurance, reporting, continuous improvement, automation, and AI.  Collaborative, student-centred and solutions-focused, with confidence to challenge established practice constructively.  Exceptional communicator with strong attention to detail, accuracy and student information systems literacy.  Qualifications   Relevant postgraduate or professional qualification in education, higher education, business, governance, public administration, law or related discipline.  Certificate IV in Training and Assessment (TAE40110 or TAE40116), or equivalent contemporary credential, is desirable where relevant.  Professional qualification or certification in academic governance, compliance, risk, quality, or public administration is desirable.    To read more about the role please click here .   This is a permanent, full-time position. The preferred location for this role is at our Surry Hills campus in Sydney. Why join us?   Bold, modern and agile, we are Australia's fastest-growing university, going from 164 students in 2014 to 40,000+ alumni across 115+ countries and 2,000+ staff today. In 2020, Australian Financial Review named us one of the country's most innovative companies. As we continue to establish ourselves as a truly global university, we never lose sight of our student-centric approach. Supported by teams of passionate people, you will find opportunities for professional development and career progression.  We are proud to be an equal opportunity employer and committed to creating an inclusive workplace. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, colour, religion, age, ethnicity, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, or any other protected characteristic.  We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all positions.  What we offer:   Study free in a course or degree related to your position.  B Corp certified university - values driven and purpose led.  Access to internal opportunities - be supported to learn, grow and move across the organisation.  A collaborative, student-focused environment where you can help shape the future of academic services.    Hiring process   We aim to provide you with the information you need at each stage of this process to help you present your best self.  If you have any accessibility requirements, please contact your recruiter at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au for confidential support. We are committed to ensuring an equitable, barrier-free application process and have a Reasonable Adjustment Procedure in place to support you throughout the recruitment process.  To learn more about what makes Torrens University Australia a great place to work, visit https://www.torrens.edu.au/blog .    We’d love to hear from you.  Job reference: R31166  To ensure a fair and thorough review process, applications for this role will close on Wednesday 4th November.    Please note, we may close applications earlier should a suitable candidate be identified. You can expect to hear from our Talent Acquisition team after this date with updates regarding your application. If you require a reasonable accommodation to complete our application process, please contact our Human Resources Department at yourcareer@torrens.edu.au"
    }
  },
